Ordered mesoporous materials (OMMs) have received increasing attention in the past decades thanks to their distinctive physical and chemical proprieties.[1] Rational pore structure and suitable pore size are crucial to determine their specific applications.[2,3] Template strategy is widely recognized as a flexible and efficient avenue to produce ordered mesostructure. Since the breakthrough of using polymeric surfactant as a soft directing agent,[4] various large-pore OMMs, such as SBA-15 or mesoporous metal oxides, were successfully achieved.[3,5] Compared to these traditional matrixes, the recently booming MOFs possess better textural designability and variability, especially at molecular level. Meanwhile, their diverse compositions and abundant active sites make them singularly attractive in adsorption, energy conversion, catalysis as well as biomedicine sciences.[6] In most of this series of applications, free mass transport is a decisive factor, so that the introduction of large and connected pore channels in MOFs matrix is usually conducive to improving their performances.[7] Nevertheless, mesoMOFs built from soft templates featuring ordered mesoporous structure, large mesopore size matching with the dimensions of most biomacromolecules $(>\textsf{6}\mathsf{n m})$ , excellent channel connectivity and robust crystallinity are rarely, if any, reported.[8]
The key issue in the templating process is that the surfactant micelles are frequently expelled from the MOFs growth domain due to the weak interaction between surfactants and growing MOFs, making it difficult to guide MOFs crystal growth. Meanwhile, most stable MOFs are normally synthesized in organic phase and harsh reaction conditions, which are unfavorable to the selfassembly of surfactant into ordered micelles. Due to these restrictions, no successful work, to the best of our knowledge, has been so far reported on the synthesis of large-pore crystallized mesoMOFs templated by polymeric surfactants. Fortunately, the Hofmeister effect, first noted in 1888,[9] provides us a new perspective for overcoming these inherent limitations. Our ongoing research demonstrated that Hofmeister ions had an important effect on the growth process of MOFs.[10] A series of salting-in ions can facilitate the crystallization of MOFs through a unique “solubilization-mediating” mechanism so that many stable MOFs could be obtained in aqueous phase and mild conditions, which are compatible for the self-assembly of soft templates. These early studies strongly inspire us to develop a rational polymeric surfactant self-assembly strategy to obtain ordered large-pore mesoMOFs with excellent chemical stability based on the Hofmeister effect.
Herein, by coupling the Hofmeister effect with the structure directing properties of triblock copolymers, we demonstrate a saltmediated template strategy to synthesize hierarchical mesoporous Ce-based MOFs (denoted as Ce-HMMOFs) with ordered, accessible large mesopores (ca. $10\ n\mathrm{m};$ and well crystallized microporous pore walls by employing Pluronic poly(ethylene oxide)-poly(propylene oxide)-poly-(ethylene oxide)s (PEO-PPO-PEO) copolymers as soft templates, 1,4- dicarboxybenzene (BDC) as framework-building ligand and Hofmeister salting-in ion of $C l O_{4}^{-}$ as a mediator. Meanwhile, by changing the templates, well-ordered hexagonal Ce-HMMOFs (named as Ce-HMMOF-P) and cubic Ce-HMMOFs (named as Ce-HMMOF-F) were obtained by use of P123 $({\mathsf{P E O}}_{20}{\mathsf{P P O}}_{70}{\mathsf{P E O}}_{20})$ and F127 $({\mathsf{P E O}}_{106}{\mathsf{P P O}}_{70}{\mathsf{P E O}}_{106})$ as structure-directing agents, respectively. Besides, the large pore channels, together with their robust microporous crystalline walls, make Ce-HMMOFs ideal candidates for bio-related applications such as working as a bioreactor.[11] Trypsin was chosen as a model enzyme to be immobilized into the Ce-HMMOFs with high capacities and subsequently exemplified to serve as a catalyst for the highly efficient digestion of a variety of biogenic proteins.
The transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images provide more details about the mesostructure of the particles (Figures 1d and S2b). On smaller particles, highly open and orderly arranged tubular channels can be observed, which are interlaced and oriented in different directions. Representative high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) image (Figure 1e) of Ce-HMMOF-P supports that the walls are indeed crystallized in good agreement with the deduction from the XRD pattern. The nitrogen sorption isotherm and its corresponding pore size distribution are shown in Figure
1f. The sample exhibits a combination of type I and type IV isotherms with a $\mathsf{H}_{1}$ hysteresis loop in the partial pressure range from 0.6 to 0.8, implying the simultaneous existence of micropores and uniform large cylindrical mesopores in CeHMMOF-P (Figure 1f (1)). The pore diameter is determined to be about $10.1\ \mathsf{n m}$ derived from the adsorption branch by using Barrett-Joyner-Halenda (BJH) method, and the pore wall thickness is calculated to be about $6.2\:\mathrm{nm}$ . The total BET surface area is around $1180~\mathsf{m}^{2}~\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , of which the mesopore surface area is $344\mathsf{m}^{2}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ . The total pore volume is around $0.89\mathsf{c m}^{3}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ .
A reasonable mechanism was deduced by a series of control experiments and summarized in Figure 2. In the first stage, Pluronic copolymers aggregate in $(N H_{4})_{2}C e(N O_{3})_{6}/a c e t$ ic $\mathsf{a c i d/N a C l O_{4}}$ aqueous solution and form rod-like micelles with PPO block as a core surrounded by PEO coronas. Since the PEO segments could form “crown-ether-type” complexes with multivalent metal species through coordination bonds,[13] the periphery of the cylindrical micelles would be surrounded by Ceoxo clusters (Step 1). Therefore, the MOFs could crystallize around the micelles along the anchored Ce clusters. Acetic acid (HAc) was introduced as a modulator to control the rates of nucleation and crystal growth by competing with linkers.[14] It is noteworthy that this competition would also affect the weak coordination between template and metal species. Therefore, the amount of HAc needs to be precisely controlled in a suitable range, because insufficient modulator would lead to the formation of low crystallinity products (Figure S3a, 3c), while excessive modulator would coordinate with tetravalent Ce and consequently weaken the interaction between metal precursors and PEO segments, thus is unfavorable to the formation of ordered mesostructure (Figure S3b, 3d).
The key to the success of assembly process lies in the role of Hofmeister salting-in ion of $C l O_{4}^{-}$ played. Recent consensus has achieved that salting-in ions are readily polarized and present a high affinity towards the hydrophobic moieties of the organic solutes through direct dipole-dipole interactions.[15,16] Therefore, after BDC ligands were added, $N a C l O_{4}$ would accumulate around the ligands, weakening their hydrophobic aggregation and thereby improving their aqueous solubility (solubilization).[15] Simultaneously, the abundant $C|\mathsf{O}_{4}^{-}$ ions would attract and bridge Ce nodes to the BDC ligands (mediating).[10] The local concentration of the MOF precursors would increase by the mediation of $C|\mathsf{O}_{4}^{-}$ ions, which promoted the growth of the crystallized MOFs around the Ce-PEO complexes coronas (Step 2). The experimental scenarios agree on our proposal that no mesoporous structure could be obtained in the absence of $C_{1}\mathrm{O}_{4}^{-}$ (Figure S4), and the pore ordering and the crystallinities of pore walls of Ce-HMMOF-P were improved with the increase of $C|\mathsf{O}_{4}^{-}$ /Ce feed ratio varying from 0 to 3.6. Moreover, it has been demonstrated that Hofmeister series has an important effect on the phase behavior of block copolymers and futher on the formation process of OMMs.[17] The specific role of these ions depends on the type of templates and pH of the reaction mixture.[17d-h] For instance, under the strong-acidic conditions for the synthesis of SBA-15, the salting-in ions are expected to be absorbed into the EO part of the polymer, thereby decreasing the diameter of hydrophobic core and increasing the corona thickness of the micelles. Meanwhile, salting-in anions promote the silica species to penetrate deeper into the micellar core, which leads to a more compact structure.[17i] Similarly, in our strongly acidic synthesis mixture $\left(\mathsf{p}\mathsf{H}<0.5\right)$ ), with the increasing of $C|\mathsf{O}_{4}\cdot$ , the mesopore size of Ce-HMMOF-P was shrunk from 12.5 to $9.3\mathsf{n m}$ (Figure S5).However, when the feed ratio of ${\mathsf{C l O}}_{4}{\mathsf{^{-}}}/{\mathsf{C e}}$ was set higher than 3.6, the excessive salting-in ion of $C|\mathsf{O}_{4}^{-}$ would bridge discrete Ce-micelle composites together and precipitate them from the aqueous solution in the form of large aggregation. Along with the precisely modulating of the coordination equilibrium by HAc and $C|\mathsf{O}_{4}^{-}$ , MOFs would grow around PEO coronas and gradually pack together to form periodically arranged hexagonal mesostructures (Step 3), and further spontaneously organized into energy-favorable spherical morphology (Step 4). Finally, the accessible large pores could be obtained by extraction procedure. FTIR spectra show that almost all the templates have been removed from the Ce-HMMOFs (Figure S6). The TGA profile indicates that the sample is stable up to $300~^{\circ}C$ , which is consistent with the thermal stability of microporous Ce-UiO-66,[12] meanwhile, the number of linker deficiencies of per ${\mathsf{C e}}_{6}$ formula unit is calculated to be about 0.89 based on the TGA data. (Figure S7).[18]
To further verify the templating effect of P123 on the assembly of Ce-HMMOF-P, synthesis temperature was systematically varied as it is well-known that temperature affects the amphiphilic property of PEO-PPO-PEO copolymers and their structuredirecting behavior.[19] When the synthesis temperature was set at $25^{\circ}C$ , the pore structure of Ce-HMMOF-P was disordered with wide pore size distribution (Figures S8a, S8d and S8g). As the temperature was raised to $35^{\circ}C$ , an ordered hexagonal structure with excellent pore size uniformity began to form (Figure 1). Upon increasing the temperature to $40~^{\circ}C$ , the more hydrophobic P123 tended to form the mesostructure with larger packing parameter, thus the Ce-HMMOF-P with multilamellar vesicle (MLV) structure was evolved (Figures S8b, S8e and S8h).[20] Further rising temperature to $45^{\circ}C$ , MLV structure was still preserved but with larger layer spacing, and the pore size distribution became much wider and the BJH average pore size enlarged to about $18.8\mathsf{n m}$ (Figures S8c, S8f and S8i). Taking together, the temperaturedependent phase evolution of the Ce-HMMOF-P indeed confirms the templating effect of triblock copolymer, which synergistically work with Hofmeister effect of salting-in ion to ensure the successful generation of ordered large-pore mesoMOFs.
In summary, a Hofmeister salting-in ion mediated template strategy was proposed to synthesize ordered large-pore mesoMOFs. During the formation process, salting-in ion is speculated to bridge the organic ligands toward metallic-nodeslinked PEO coronas of triblock copolymers, thus enabling the directed growth and subsequent generation of well-crystallized MOFs along surfactant micelles in aqueous phase. With different templates of P123 and F127, large-pore mesoMOFs contain hexagonal and cubic mesostructures with thick microporous walls are successfully obtained, indicative of the extensibility of the developed strategy. The enzyme immobilization and its subsequent efficient digestion of various biogenic proteins exemplify the potential applications of the Ce-HMMOFs with biomacromolecules involved. We expect that the present strategy would provide new insights into the preparation of a large variety of large-pore mesoMOFs with different compositions and functionalities.
